From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 19:48:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] Add narration slicing and exact shot trimming
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two primitives the character lip-sync pass needs, landed on their own
so they can be tested without it.

sliceNarration cuts one span out of a narration track as a standalone
WAV. A lip-sync model must receive the audio for exactly the shot it
is syncing — give it the whole track and the mouth follows words
belonging to a different shot. Cue timings already come from byte
offsets into this same PCM, so slicing on those offsets is exact to
the sample rather than approximate: the audio a shot receives is the
audio the timeline plays under it. Offsets snap to whole frames,
out-of-range spans clamp, and an empty or inverted span yields a valid
silent WAV rather than a malformed file the model would choke on.

trimClipToStart is enforceClipDuration's stricter sibling, for footage
about to be synced. It drops the tolerance window, because a lip-sync
model takes video and audio as separate files and assumes they start
together — the third of a second enforceClipDuration ignores is a
third of a second of wrong mouth. And it never seeks: providers return
discrete lengths, so a 3.2s shot arrives as a 5s clip, and the surplus
comes off the end rather than being sampled from the middle. It is
deliberately not fail-soft; handing the model a mismatched clip
produces a confidently desynced result, which is worse than a failed
job.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
